    <exercise number="50" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Use the following steps to show that</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{\left( {- 1} \right)^{n - 1}}{n} = \ln 2$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Let $$h_{n}$$ and $$s_{n}$$ be the partial sums of the harmonic and alternating harmonic series.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Show that $$s_{2n} = h_{2n} - h_{n}$$ .</item>
          <item>From Exercise 11.3.46 we have $$\left. h_{n} - \ln n\rightarrow\gamma {as} \right.\left. n\rightarrow\infty \right.$$ and therefore $$\left. h_{2n} - \ln\left( {2n} \right)\rightarrow\gamma {as} \right.\left. n\rightarrow\infty \right.$$ Use these facts together with part (a) to show that $$\left. s_{2n}\rightarrow\ln 2 \right.$$ as $$\left. n\rightarrow\infty \right.$$ .</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
